  <!-- ══════════ 1 · WHAT THE FLOOR IS ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="what-the-floor-is">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The line</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">What the floor is</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">The floor is the lower-bound line of bitcoin&rsquo;s price history over the long term &mdash; a lower edge the price has held with remarkable consistency and fit, to the point where &ldquo;floor&rdquo; is the accurate description. That consistency is in sharp contrast with the upper edge, where price action has been far more volatile, with brief violent spikes.</p>

    <div class="fl-chart-block">
      <div class="fl-chart-head">
        <div>
          <div class="fl-chart-title" id="flChannelTitle">Bitcoin against the Power Law channel</div>
          <div class="fl-chart-sub" id="flChannelSub">Log-log. The floor is drawn solid; trend and upper band are secondary. Shaded bands mark the four approaches to the floor.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-chart-wrapper chart-wrapper" data-chart-copy
           data-chart-heading="#flChannelTitle"
           data-chart-sub="#flChannelSub"
           data-chart-title="The Bitcoin Floor — the channel, floor emphasised"
           data-chart-filename="bitcoin-floor-channel.png">
        <canvas id="flChannelChart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <p class="fl-chart-note">Hover any point for trend, floor and price together. <span class="fl-tip" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-describedby="flTipExp">Why the floor&rsquo;s exponent is the trend&rsquo;s<span class="fl-tip-body" id="flTipExp" role="tooltip">The floor is defined as a constant multiple of trend, so its exponent is 5.77 by construction &mdash; not by fit. Fitted independently, the record&rsquo;s lower edge comes out slightly steeper: 5.8834 at the 5th percentile, 5.9552 at the 2nd, and 5.8999 through the eight cycle lows. Three methods, all near 5.88&ndash;5.96. Over sixteen years that gap is small; extrapolated forward it compounds in log space, which is the caveat that matters on any long projection.</span></span></p>
    </div>

    <div class="fl-prose">
      <p>The Power Law trend is a line through bitcoin&rsquo;s price history in log-log space. The floor is that same line, shifted down: <strong>0.42&times; the trend price, at the same exponent</strong>. Because the exponent is shared, the two run parallel forever rather than converging &mdash; the floor is a statement about <em>level</em>, not about slope. That is a property of how the channel is defined, not something measured.</p>

      <p>What <em>can</em> be measured is whether the price history&rsquo;s own lower edge runs parallel to that line. It does &mdash; and it sits slightly above it.</p>

      <p>Fitting the lower edge independently &mdash; quantile regressions at the 2nd through 10th percentiles, each free to choose its own slope &mdash; produces exponents clustered in a narrow band of <strong>5.86 to 5.96</strong>, against the trend exponent of <strong>5.77</strong> that the site uses. Every quantile from the 2nd to the median lands between 5.79 and 5.96: support for the <em>form</em> of the model, a constant-multiple channel rather than one that fans open or shut with time.</p>

      <p>The second finding is the one that matters for a reader standing at the floor. A <strong>5th-percentile line</strong> is a line only 5% of days closed beneath &mdash; the usual way to draw a &ldquo;lower bound&rdquo; that still admits the occasional exception. Fitted on this record, that line would sit near <strong>0.46&times; trend</strong>. The published 0.42&times; ratio sits <em>below</em> it, at roughly the <strong>2nd</strong> percentile: only about 2% of days closed under it.</p>

      <p>So <strong>the published ratio is stricter than its own description</strong>. The 0.42&times; is the <a href="https://www.porkopolis.io/thechart/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Porkopolis</a> calibration this site adopts, not a judgment call made here &mdash; and the data alone would have put a lower bound about 10% higher. Whatever else the floor is, it is not drawn flatteringly.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 2 · EPISODE EXPLORER ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="the-four-approaches">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The record</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">The closest approaches &mdash; and what followed</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">Price rides near this line as its ordinary lower bound; that continuity is the finding, not any single event. The four approaches below are the closest it has come &mdash; case studies, not violations. What makes them worth studying is the roundtrip: <strong>every modern approach sat above trend within 24 months, three out of three</strong>.</p>

    <div class="fl-prose">
      <p>The three modern approaches went under the line by <strong>1.8%</strong>, <strong>5.1%</strong> and <strong>0.4%</strong>, and none of them held there. The fourth sits in 2010, in bitcoin&rsquo;s genesis era, and went <strong>42.6%</strong> under. Treating that one as equal evidence about the modern floor would be a statistical error &mdash; it comes from the same pre-maturity period every careful fit of this model down-weights. It is recorded below for completeness, once, and carries no weight in anything this page concludes.</p>

      <p><strong>A note on duration, which applies to every figure here.</strong> The historical series samples roughly every 12 days, so a &ldquo;single-sample&rdquo; approach means the true stay below the line could be anything under about 24 days. Every duration on this page is a lower bound, and none of them can see intraday moves at all.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="fl-zoom-bar" id="flZoomBar">
      <span class="fl-zoom-context" id="flZoomContext">Showing the full history.</span>
      <button type="button" class="fl-zoom-reset" id="flZoomReset" hidden>Show full history</button>
    </div>

    <div class="fl-ep-strip" id="flEpStrip" role="tablist" aria-label="Floor approaches"></div>
    <div class="fl-ep-card" id="flEpCard" role="tabpanel" aria-live="polite"></div>

    <div class="fl-stat-row" id="flRevStats">
      <div class="fl-stat">
        <div class="fl-stat-v" id="flRevMedian">&mdash;</div>
        <div class="fl-stat-k">median share of the gap to trend closed within 24 months. 100% would be a return to trend exactly; above 100% means price overshot past it</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-stat">
        <div class="fl-stat-v" id="flRevOvershoot">&mdash;</div>
        <div class="fl-stat-k">modern approaches closed the gap entirely and sat above trend within 24 months</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="fl-caveat">The four followed different paths in year one &mdash; at 12 months their outcomes have nothing in common. By 24 months they agree. That is the whole of what the reversion evidence says, and it says it about four approaches in a series whose own coefficients were fitted on substantially this same history.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 3 · THE PARITY INSTRUMENT ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="what-the-floor-paid">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The measurement</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">How entering at the floor has paid off</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">The same entries, two defensible endpoints, two different stories &mdash; and both are true. What &ldquo;did the floor pay&rdquo; means depends entirely on where you stop measuring, so this instrument makes you choose.</p>

    <div class="fl-endpoint-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Measurement endpoint" data-toggle-group>
      <span class="fl-endpoint-label">Measure to:</span>
      <button type="button" class="fl-seg-btn is-active" data-endpoint="today" aria-pressed="true">Graded to today</button>
      <button type="button" class="fl-seg-btn" data-endpoint="trend" aria-pressed="false">Graded to the last time price touched the trend line</button>
    </div>

    <div class="fl-parity-card" id="flParityCard">
      <div class="fl-parity-grid">
        <div class="fl-parity-cell">
          <div class="fl-parity-k">Realized CAGR</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-v" id="flParityRealized">&mdash;</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-sub" id="flParityRealizedSub">what the entries earned</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fl-parity-cell fl-parity-op"><span aria-hidden="true">&minus;</span></div>
        <div class="fl-parity-cell">
          <div class="fl-parity-k">Trend-line CAGR, same windows</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-v" id="flParityTrend">&mdash;</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-sub">what the trend line grew</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fl-parity-cell fl-parity-op"><span aria-hidden="true">&asymp;</span></div>
        <div class="fl-parity-cell fl-parity-cell-excess">
          <div class="fl-parity-k" id="flParityExcessK">The difference</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-v" id="flParityExcess">&mdash;</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-sub" id="flParityExcessSub">median of the per-entry differences &mdash; a bonus over the trend line&rsquo;s own growth when positive, a shortfall when negative</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fl-parity-method" id="flParityMethod">&mdash;</p>

      <div class="fl-parity-read" id="flParityRead"></div>

      <div class="fl-honesty">
        <div class="fl-honesty-item">
          <div class="fl-honesty-k">The vintage caveat</div>
          <p id="flHonestyVintage">These CAGRs are enormous because the median entry dates from bitcoin&rsquo;s higher-growth past. The model&rsquo;s own arithmetic declines with age: an entry at the floor today implies far less forward growth than an entry at the floor in 2015 did, and nothing here should be read as the rate a floor entry would earn from here.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="fl-honesty-item">
          <div class="fl-honesty-k">The endpoint caveat</div>
          <p id="flHonestyEndpoint">&mdash;</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="fl-parity-foot">The difference &mdash; the extra return vs the trend line &mdash; is the median of each entry&rsquo;s own realized-minus-trend figure &mdash; not the difference of the two medians above, which is a different and less meaningful number. Windows run from each entry date to the selected endpoint; CAGRs annualise on a 365.25-day year.</p>
    </div>

  </section>

