  <!-- ══════════ 4 · THE TRIPWIRE ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="the-tripwire">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The falsifier</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">What would break the Power Law model for bitcoin?</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">These criteria are subjective, but they are defensible and are based on the historical record.</p>

    <div class="fl-tripwire" id="flTripwire">
      <div class="fl-tripwire-head">Model break criteria</div>
      <p class="fl-tripwire-crit">The Power Law model is failing its own test if price sustains a close <strong>more than 10% below the floor</strong> for <strong>more than 30 consecutive days</strong>.</p>
      <p class="fl-tripwire-why"><strong>Both conditions have to hold at once.</strong> Depth alone is not a break &mdash; a single day 11% under is a wick, not a failure &mdash; and duration alone is not either, since a month spent 2% under the line is the floor doing its job. It is the two together that separate a real break from a graze, and the record is what sets them there: the three modern approaches never met the pair, the deepest going 5.1% under and none of them holding. The genesis-era 2010 episode exceeded both at once, on every measure available in the historical series &mdash; which is one reason the criteria sit where they do, and not a reason to treat that episode as evidence about today.</p>
      <p class="fl-tripwire-why"><strong>A note on what is measured, and how.</strong> The historical record above is read from a series that samples roughly every 12 days, so its durations are lower bounds. The tripwire is not: from ship date forward it is judged on <strong>daily closes</strong>, live. The two are deliberately different instruments &mdash; the history is what can be reconstructed, the tripwire is what can be watched.</p>

      <div class="fl-tripwire-status" id="flTripwireStatus">
        <div class="fl-tripwire-status-k">Status today</div>
        <div class="fl-tripwire-status-v" id="flTripwireStatusV">&mdash;</div>
      </div>

      <p class="fl-tripwire-ceiling">Secondary watch, the other direction: a sustained break <em>above</em> the 3&times; ceiling, beyond the brief cyclical visits the record already contains, would not falsify the model so much as suggest the denominator had changed &mdash; that the thing being priced, or the money pricing it, is no longer the same measurement.</p>

      <p class="fl-tripwire-commit">If the tripwire fires &mdash; a close more than 10% below the floor, held for more than 30 consecutive days &mdash; this page will say so the same day, in this box.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 5 · THE OTHER SIDE ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="the-other-side">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">Both sides</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">The other side of this</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">Four reasons to hold everything above more loosely than the precision of the numbers suggests.</p>

    <div class="fl-otherside">
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">The one sub-floor episode &mdash; and why it isn&rsquo;t evidence</div>
        <p>In autumn 2010 price closed 42.6% below the line and stayed under for at least 48 days. That is the only time the floor has been genuinely breached, and this is the one place on the page it gets prose.</p>
        <p>It sits in bitcoin&rsquo;s genesis era: no mature exchange, negligible liquidity, a price measured in cents, and a market thin enough that a single participant could move it. That is the same period every careful fit of this model down-weights, for the same reason. Treating it as evidence about the modern floor would be a statistical error &mdash; it is recorded for completeness, not for weight, and nothing this page concludes rests on it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">If adoption saturates, the floor breaks before anything else does</div>
        <p>The strongest argument against the Power Law is that it describes an adoption curve which must eventually flatten &mdash; an S-curve caught mid-rise looks like a power law right up until it doesn&rsquo;t. The tripwire is exactly the instrument that would register saturation as it began to bite, because a flattening adoption curve shows up first as a floor that stops holding. See <a href="/the-power-law">the Power Law page</a> for the model&rsquo;s limits in full.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">A floor test can fail for denominator reasons</div>
        <p>Every figure here is a ratio with dollars underneath it. A break of the floor would be evidence about bitcoin; it could also be evidence about the dollar, or about the price series, or about which exchanges the series draws from. The test is not clean, and no single test of a monetary thesis is.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">Nothing here is an entry signal</div>
        <p>&ldquo;At the floor&rdquo; is a location, not a verdict about value. Graded floor-to-floor, the measurement above found that entering there did not beat the trend line &mdash; and the whole entry set is 26 samples clustered in a handful of stretches, in a series whose own coefficients were fitted on much the same history. Position size is the tool that survives being wrong about all of this &mdash; see <a href="/bitcoin-allocation-sizing">Bitcoin Portfolio Allocation</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

